                <pb facs="0157" n="150" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg150"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/8730: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-13T09:00:00+08:00">July 13, 1937—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 13—5:02 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>272. Our 270, July 12, midnight.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>The First Secretary of the British Embassy received last night the same
                        message from Timperley and subsequently made inquiry of the Foreign Office
                        whether the message represented the Foreign Office’s views. Dr. T. T. Li,
                        Director of the Department of Intelligence and Publicity of the Ministry,
                        informed him that such was the case and that “it was the wish of the Chinese
                        Government” that the British and American Ambassadors come to Nanking. The
                        British Secretary has telegraphed this information to Peiping and to his
                        Ambassador at Peitaiho.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Sent to the Department, Peiping.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Peck</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
